1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,580
Smoke is not a monolithic thing. Smoke is a mixture of gases, liquids, and primarily

2
00:00:05,580 --> 00:00:10,160
particles, which are also known as aerosols. Paces gather data on these

3
00:00:10,160 --> 00:00:14,640
aerosols so that we can get a better picture of how smoke interacts with the

4
00:00:14,640 --> 00:00:20,320
atmosphere and all kinds of far-reaching effects. What a fire emits is highly

5
00:00:20,320 --> 00:00:25,640
variable and it can depend on the number of factors such as fuel, moisture, and

6
00:00:25,680 --> 00:00:30,720
the efficiency of the combustion. An efficiently burning fire can have dark

7
00:00:30,720 --> 00:00:37,230
colored smoke, releasing a lot of carbon monoxide and carbon dioxide. A less

8
00:00:37,230 --> 00:00:41,630
efficient burning fire can have different colored smoke, a lighter colored smoke,

9
00:00:41,630 --> 00:00:46,430
and in addition to the carbon monoxide and carbon dioxide also releases a

10
00:00:46,430 --> 00:00:51,190
number of other chemical compounds into the atmosphere. Soot, or black carbon,

11
00:00:51,190 --> 00:00:56,190
is a result of efficient burning, and soot consists of chains of elemental

12
00:00:56,190 --> 00:01:00,190
carbon sort of like graphite. Because they're dark, soot particles absorb

13
00:01:00,190 --> 00:01:05,630
energy from the Sun and warm up the air surrounding the particle. Emitted

14
00:01:05,630 --> 00:01:10,710
alongside black carbon are volatile organic compounds. These are gases which

15
00:01:10,710 --> 00:01:15,710
can condense downwind from a fire onto the soot. These created particles are

16
00:01:15,710 --> 00:01:20,030
called secondary organic aerosols, which means that they were not emitted

17
00:01:20,030 --> 00:01:24,310
at the source. Secondary organic aerosols also form when smoke

18
00:01:24,430 --> 00:01:29,310
interacts with the atmosphere. So one way in which secondary organic aerosols are

19
00:01:29,310 --> 00:01:34,590
formed is through photochemical reactions. That means sunlight interacts with

20
00:01:34,590 --> 00:01:39,830
gases emitted from smoke and those gases condense to form either particles in

21
00:01:39,830 --> 00:01:45,150
their own right or form onto existing particles such as the soot which was

22
00:01:45,150 --> 00:01:50,110
emitted originally. Another type of aerosol from fires is called brown

23
00:01:50,110 --> 00:01:55,270
carbon, and in this case the smoke is, as one could expect, brownish in color,

24
00:01:55,270 --> 00:01:59,910
meaning it's absorbing more light towards the blue and ultraviolet portion of the

25
00:01:59,910 --> 00:02:05,070
electromagnetic spectrum. Brown carbon is formed when vegetation burns and

26
00:02:05,070 --> 00:02:10,910
incorporated into the smoke are what are called humic substances and these

27
00:02:10,910 --> 00:02:15,470
are the remnants of vegetation material. Imagine the pigments that are in

28
00:02:15,470 --> 00:02:22,550
leaves as they decompose. All of these different smoke components have different

29
00:02:22,550 --> 00:02:26,870
optical properties, and these optical properties describe how those particles

30
00:02:26,870 --> 00:02:33,460
interact with light. The instruments on pace can measure some of those

31
00:02:33,460 --> 00:02:37,700
properties, including at different wavelengths of light, at different

32
00:02:37,700 --> 00:02:42,460
geometries and angles, and from that data we can figure out the quantity, the

33
00:02:42,460 --> 00:02:47,700
color, the composition, the shape, and the size of aerosols in the

34
00:02:47,700 --> 00:02:52,660
atmosphere, including smoke aerosols. We use all of that information together and

35
00:02:52,660 --> 00:02:57,420
we use that to differentiate between smoke and other types of particles in the

36
00:02:57,420 --> 00:03:03,500
atmosphere such as dust or different types of smoke from each other. The data

37
00:03:03,500 --> 00:03:08,740
from PACE will help inform wildfire models but also give us a general better

38
00:03:08,740 --> 00:03:13,500
sense of how aerosols from smoke impact things like air quality, human

39
00:03:13,500 --> 00:03:20,020
health, weather, cloud formation, and other aspects of how our planet Earth works.

